{"t":1794921774,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"24486a01-e98d-414d-a3cc-74c620c11cea","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-11-17T08:29:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 170829\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n329 AM EST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nAdded a key message to address the potential low pressure system\nthat could possibly impact the region Sunday into Monday. \n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Dry and warm conditions expected through the end of the \n  week.\n\n- 2) Increasing confidence in a low pressure system developing \n  in the southeast this weekend, potentially impacting our \n  region Sunday into Monday. \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry and warm conditions expected through the end of\nthe week.\n\nHigh pressure will build over the central Plains in the upper \nlevels, while transitioning to a surface high pressure over the \nmid-Atlantic. This ridge will result in very dry conditions and \ngradually increasing temperatures through the rest of the week. \nPlenty of sunshine each day with highs approaching the middle to\nupper 60s to near 70 by Friday across the region. Lows at night\nwill remain in the 30s to 40s through the week. One caveat to \nthe prolonged dry conditions and warming temperatures could have\nimplications on fire weather (see Fire Weather section below). \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Increasing confidence in a low pressure system \ndeveloping in the southeast this weekend, potentially impacting \nour region Sunday into Monday. \n\nA piece of shortwave energy, loosely connected to the trough \nthat moved offshore last week, will be spinning over the \nsoutheast CONUS/Gulf Coast region. This likely gets pushed off \nto our south and east with little impact towards the end of the \nweek. Depending on what model guidance you look at though, this\ncould even play a role later in the week, so thought I'd mention\nit here just to have it in the broader picture. What is more \ninteresting to watch for our area is what comes in behind it. \nThe subtropical jet shows signs of becoming active during this \nlate week/weekend timeframe. Virtually all guidance now develops\na closed upper low somewhere along the Gulf Coast/Southeast \nAtlantic coast by late Saturday/early Sunday. There will also be\na very potent upper trough and attendant surface low across the\ncentral CONUS, which is currently over the Gulf of Alaska. The \nevolution of this low pressure system will play a pivotal role \nin where the low over the southeast coast ends up going. \n\nThis is where details get very murky. Without getting into the\nspecifics of each model, there is a solution that shows about \nany low track you could think of. The ultimate track of the low\nis going to depend on exactly when and where it forms, which is\njust information we do not have at this point. Ensemble guidance\nis all over the place, with tracks ranging from up the\nAppalachian mountains to well offshore. For now, this is just \nsomething to monitor closely in the coming days. \n\nIt is important to note that a majority of the relevant pieces \nof energy that need to develop for this system to form are \nstill not being well-sampled into model data, as they remain \noffshore or haven't developed at all. However, that will change\nover the next 24 to 36 hours, so stay tuned to the latest\nforecast as this potential system unfolds. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /07Z TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nMainly dry for all TAF sites through the upcoming week. VFR \nconditions are anticipated through the end of the week as high \npressure remains in control. Skies should remain mostly clear, \nwith winds staying below 10 knots generally. Winds generally \nflow WSW before shifting northwesterly Friday.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nWinds become light and variable to calm at times through much \nof this week. Winds are expected to shift southerly by Thursday\nnight before shifting back to the north on Friday, generally \nstaying below 10 knots.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nHigh pressure gradually builds overhead through the middle of the\nweek, then temperatures will begin to warm mid to late week. \nDuring this timeframe, while winds will diminish, RH values will \ngo down substantially. The dry air really builds in starting \ntoday into tonight, and looks to continue for several days \nthereafter. Overnight recoveries, especially on the ridges, will \nlikely be very poor as well starting tonight. Expect afternoon RH\nvalues to drop well into the teens and 20s each day west of the \nBlue Ridge, and the 20s to 30s east of the Blue Ridge. This will \nalso be paired with seasonably warm temperatures, so this will \nreally act to dry out already very dry fuels.\n\nNot helping matters is that there is no precipitation expected \neast of the Appalachians through at least the end of the week. \nGiven the setup, will really be watching for any potential dry \nfrontal passages in the long range as the next days to really \nwatch out for. Watching late weekend into early next week for the\nnext weather system, but unsure what it will bring at this point.\nWhile precipitation is possible, it could also bring a dry frontal\npassage instead. Continue to monitor the latest forecast as this\ntimeframe approaches. \n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...None.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...CJL/SRT\nAVIATION...CJL/SRT\nMARINE...CJL/SRT\n"},"forecast":{"t":1794921713,"days":{"2026-11-17":{"hi":61,"lo":38,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-11-18":{"hi":65,"lo":37,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-11-19":{"hi":67,"lo":42,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-11-20":{"hi":69,"lo":44,"pop":0,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-11-21":{"hi":66,"lo":49,"pop":15,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-11-22":{"hi":67,"lo":49,"pop":25,"short":"Chance Light Rain"},"202